  "prediction": "The report outlines the Atomic Energy Act (AEA) of 1954, as amended, and its provisions regarding civilian nuclear cooperation agreements between the United States and other countries. The AEA sets out the process for Congressional approval of such agreements, which includes a 90-day review period and the possibility of joint resolutions of approval or disapproval.\n\nThe report also discusses various instances where nuclear cooperation agreements have been approved outside of the AEA process, including with China, India, and Norway. In these cases, Presidents have relied on specific provisions of the AEA or other legislation to bypass the standard approval process.\n\nAdditionally, the report mentions ongoing legislative efforts to amend the AEA, including the Nuclear Cooperation Agreement Authorization Act of 2018 and the Nuclear Energy Innovation and Modernization Act of 2019. These bills aim to streamline the approval process for nuclear cooperation agreements and address various policy issues related to nuclear energy.\n\nOverall, the report provides a comprehensive overview of the legal framework for civilian nuclear cooperation agreements between the United States and other countries, as well as recent developments and ongoing debates in this area.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report outlines the Atomic Energy Act (AEA) of 1954, as amended, and its provisions regarding civilian nuclear cooperation agreements between the United States and other countries. The AEA sets out the process for Congressional approval of such agreements, which includes a 90-day review period and the possibility of joint resolutions of approval or disapproval.\n\nThe report also discusses various instances where nuclear cooperation agreements have been approved outside of the AEA process, including with China, India, and Norway. In these cases, Presidents have relied on specific provisions of the AEA or other legislation to bypass the standard approval process.\n\nAdditionally, the report mentions ongoing legislative efforts to amend the AEA, including the Nuclear Cooperation Agreement Authorization Act of 2018 and the Nuclear Energy Innovation and Modernization Act of 2019. These bills aim to streamline the approval process for nuclear cooperation agreements and address various policy issues related to nuclear energy.\n\nOverall, the report provides a comprehensive overview of the legal framework for civilian nuclear cooperation agreements between the United States and other countries, as well as recent developments and ongoing debates in this area.",
  "score": 23.863635892110032,
  "answers": [
    "In order for the United States to engage in significant civilian nuclear cooperation with other states, it must conclude a framework agreement that meets specific requirements under Section 123 of the Atomic Energy Act (AEA). Significant nuclear cooperation includes the export of reactors, critical parts of reactors, and reactor fuel. The AEA also provides for export control licensing procedures and criteria for terminating cooperation. Congressional review is required for Section 123 agreements; the AEA establishes special parliamentary procedures by which Congress may act on a proposed agreement."
  ],
